# Kalantar et al. (2025), JGR Solid Earth, 10.1029/2025JB031938.
# Specimen OG-SC -- SAW-CUT fracture in Odenwald granodiorite.
#
# Units: m.
# Geometry (paper Table 1): length = 0.10000, diameter = 0.04998,
# fracture angle = 30 degrees, measured from the specimen's long/vertical axis to
# the fracture plane.
#
# WHY 30 DEGREES IS TRUSTED HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Recovered independently from the paper's own Table 2, not taken from Table 1.
# Dividing equation (3) by equation (4) removes the differential stress:
#
#     tan(theta) = (sigma'_n - sigma_3 + P_p) / tau,     P_p = (P_i + 3)/2 MPa
#
# Regressing the thirteen OG-SC hold stages on that identity returns
# theta = 29.998 deg and sigma_3 = 33.002 MPa, maximum residual 0.006 MPa. Two
# constants reproduce the whole stress table. See
# scripts/kalantar_parameter_audit.py section 1.
#
# sigma_3 = 33.00 MPa is the confining stress the deck must apply: it is the
# 30 MPa EFFECTIVE confining pressure of section 2.3 plus the 3 MPa pore pressure
# that both pumps hold at the start of injection.
#
# Plane, centred at z = L/2 = 0.050, at x = +/- r = 0.02499:
#     z = L/2 +/- r cot(30 deg) = 0.050 +/- 0.043283950
#     z_high = 0.093283950      z_low = 0.006716050
# The elliptical fracture spans 86.57 mm of the 100.00 mm core, clearing each end
# face by 6.72 mm.
#
# Expected fracture_interface area after the split: pi r^2 / sin(30 deg)
#     = 3.923850e-3 m^2.
#
# THE ONE SPECIMEN WHOSE ROUGHNESS CHANGE IS MEASURABLE. The paper propagates the
# scanner's 0.012 mm resolution to +/-2.10 in JRC and concludes that the OG-SH and
# OG-T roughness changes (-0.39 and -0.29) are not statistically significant.
# OG-SC's is: JRC 4.23 -> 1.36, a change of -2.87. So this is the only specimen in
# the paper where a JRC-degradation model has an observable to fit, and the change
# is a SMOOTHING of an already smooth saw cut. Worth remembering when choosing
# which fracture to test a roughness-evolution law on.
#
# AFTER REGENERATING, pin the source coordinates onto real interface nodes.
# Section 2.3 places two 2 mm boreholes 5 mm from the sidewall, one at each end, so
# the intended points are at x = +/- (r - 0.005) = +/- 0.019990:
#     source_in   coord = '-0.019990 0 0.015376304'
#     source_out  coord = ' 0.019990 0 0.084623696'
# use_closest_node = true snaps silently, including onto bulk nodes off the
# fracture. Run scripts/check_source_nodes.py and write the exact node coordinates
# back into the deck. See the note on the 5 mm inset ambiguity in the OG-SH
# journal; it applies identically here.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-08-23 against kalantar2025_og_sc_theta30_size_3.e (68,096 elements,
# 2185 interface nodes, median interface pitch 1.004 mm). Both sources land ON the
# fracture, 388.5 um from the design point. USE THESE IN THE DECK:
#     source_in   coord = '-0.020184231 0 0.015039887'
#     source_out  coord = ' 0.020184231 0 0.084960113'
# The snap moves the modelled borehole to 4.806 mm from the sidewall (design 5.000)
# and lengthens the borehole separation to 80.7369 mm, +0.972 %. Through the
# ln(2*l/T - 1) factor of eq (7) that is a +0.224 % bias on Q -- below the level at
# which this campaign ranks anything, but it must be the value the deck's flow
# postprocessor uses, not the design value.
#
# MESH SIZE. "auto factor" is a Cubit coarseness index, not a length. Factor 5 is
# the production discretisation; factor 3 is the convergence sibling.
#
# WARNING: kalantar2025_og_sc_theta30_size5.e in this directory is byte-identical in
# content to size_3.e -- same 72,105 nodes, same 68,096 elements, same interface
# pitch. It is a stale export made before the size line was edited, NOT a factor-5
# mesh. Scoring the two against each other would be a no-op that looks like perfect
# mesh convergence. Delete it or rebuild it before any convergence claim.

reset
create Cylinder height 0.10000 radius 0.02499
move Volume 1  z 0.050 include_merged

create vertex location 0.02499 0.02499 0.093283950
create vertex location 0.02499 -0.02499 0.093283950
create vertex location -0.02499 -0.02499 0.006716050
webcut volume 1 with plane vertex 3 vertex 4 vertex 5
delete vertex 3 4 5
webcut volume all with plane xplane imprint merge
webcut volume all with plane yplane imprint merge
imprint all
merge all

# ==============================================================================
# GRADED SIZING: TRIED 2026-08-24, MEASURED, AND REJECTED. Read this before
# trying it again. The premise was wrong, not the execution.
# ==============================================================================
# The idea was to size the fracture and the bulk independently -- fracture 1.00 mm,
# bulk 1.80 mm -- on the theory that these meshes were over-refined in the BULK and a
# large fraction of the elements sat in rock where nothing happens.
#
# THEY WERE NOT. Median node spacing [mm] against distance from the fracture plane,
# measured on the exported meshes:
#
#     distance      0-1   1-2   2-4   4-8  8-16  16-32  32-100 mm
#     _size3.e     0.974 0.957 0.990 0.979 0.919 0.911  0.962
#     graded       0.946 0.909 0.950 0.922 0.861 0.860  0.912
#
# Both profiles are FLAT. The mesh was already uniform at ~0.95 mm everywhere out to
# 100 mm from the fracture; there was no over-refined bulk to recover. And the
# `volume all size` line had no effect at all -- the SURFACE size propagated through
# the entire volume and made the whole mesh finer.
#
# The reason is the element type. `scheme polyhedron` produces HEX8 here, and a hex
# mesher propagates surface intervals along the mapped directions through the whole
# volume. It cannot grade away from an interior surface the way a tet mesher can.
# Refining the fracture necessarily refines everything.
#
# WHAT IT COST ON THIS SPECIMEN, measured:
#     elements        68,096 ->  65,744   (-3.5 %, nowhere near the 50k target)
#     interface nodes  2,185 ->   2,407   (+10.2 %)
#     interface pitch  1.004 ->   0.941 mm
#     source pinning   PASSES  ->  FAILS  -- both boreholes snap to BULK nodes
#                                            742.7 um away, nearest interface node
#                                            744.4 um away
#     re-pinned separation 80.7369 -> 81.4489 mm, so
#     mesh_flow_width_over_length_og_sc moves -0.87 % on a SCORED channel, to buy a
#     3.5 % element saving that will not show up in wall time.
#
# HOW THE WRONG PREMISE WAS REACHED, so it is not repeated: the "implied bulk edge"
# was inferred as (volume/n_elements)^(1/3) and compared against the interface pitch,
# and the ratio was read as weak grading. Those two numbers are not commensurable --
# one is a 3D element-volume scale, the other a 2D in-plane node spacing -- so the
# ratio was an artefact of the comparison, not a property of the mesh. The
# spacing-versus-distance profile above is the direct measurement, it costs one
# command, and it should have been run first.
#
# IF THIS MESH EVER NEEDS TO BE CHEAPER: it cannot be done by grading with this
# scheme, only by coarsening globally. The lever is not the mesh -- use 128 ranks.
# The durable fix would be to imprint the two borehole vertices into the geometry so
# a node exists at each source BY CONSTRUCTION, making source pinning exact and
# mesh-independent and allowing free coarsening. Not attempted.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACTIVE SIZING: the production mesh, uniform, factor 3.
vol all size auto factor 3   # 5 -> kalantar2025_og_sc_theta30_size5.e ; 3 -> _size3.e
volume all scheme polyhedron
mesh volume all

# The graded pair, kept so the measurement above can be reproduced. If you uncomment
# these, change the export name at the bottom to _size3.e:
#   volume all size 0.00180
#   surface 26 46 53 32 size 0.00100

list totals   # element count

block 1 add volume 2 4 8 6
block 1 name "top_block"

block 2 add volume 1 3 5 7
block 2 name "bottom_block"

# Surface and vertex IDs follow this exact construction order and are shared by all
# four Ye2018 journals. Confirm "fracture_interface" reports the area quoted in the
# header before exporting -- a mis-numbered nodeset fails silently, not loudly.
nodeset 1 add surface 36 56 63 42
nodeset 1 name "top_nodeset"
nodeset 2 add surface 28 30 50 48
nodeset 2 name "bottom_nodeset"
nodeset 3 add surface 33 43 61 51 25 35 57 47
nodeset 3 name "sides_nodeset"
nodeset 4 add surface 26 46 53 32
nodeset 4 name "fracture_interface"
nodeset 5 add vertex 27 34 41 48
nodeset 5 name "no_disp_x"
nodeset 6 add vertex 10 11 18 19
nodeset 6 name "no_disp_y"

# VERIFY BEFORE EXPORTING, then ALWAYS run scripts/check_source_nodes.py.
list nodeset 4                          # interface nodes, expect 2185
list surface 26 46 53 32 geometry       # fracture area, expect 3.923850e-3 m^2

export mesh "kalantar2025_og_sc_theta30_size3.e" overwrite
